{id:"BG020",at:"/inference/argument/2/text",kind:"elementary",fact:"The doubly indexed W is countable, covers F, and the displayed nonnegative-series estimate follows from finite subsums without any unproved exchange of analytic limits.",grounds:[{location:"/inference/argument/2/text",reason:"i ranges over a subset of ℕ and j over 1,…,N_i, with finite N_i≥1; F002 supplies sum r_i^q<ε."},{location:"/definitions/1/definition",reason:"Nonnegative countable sums are finite-subsums suprema."},{location:"/definitions/6/definition",reason:"F consists exactly of (x,t) with x∈E and t∈[0,1]."}],justification:"Pairs (i,j) inject into ℕ via (i+j)^2+i: for fixed s=i+j the values lie in [s²,s²+s], these intervals are disjoint for distinct s, and within one s the value determines i,j. Index gaps can be padded with empty sets. For x∈E choose i with x∈U_i; the finite adjacent closed intervals [(j−1)/N_i,j/N_i] cover [0,1], including both endpoints. Their horizontal/vertical squared-distance bound gives distance≤√2 r_i by BG011–BG012 and finite ordered-field comparison, hence the same diameter bound by D002. Any finite set of pairs lies in finitely many complete finite rows. Its cost is at most the sum of the corresponding N_i(√2 r_i)^(q+1), at most C times the corresponding sum r_i^q, and at most C times the full sum. Taking finite-subsums suprema proves total cost≤C sum r_i^q<Cε. Conversely finite complete rows are finite sets of pairs, which also justifies the indicated regrouping. Empty/finite index families cause no exception."},
{id:"BG021",at:"/inference/argument/4/text",kind:"elementary",fact:"The bounds at all q>D yield the claimed endpoint dim_H(F)≤D+1 without a limit theorem at the critical exponent.",grounds:[{location:"/inputs/1/statement",reason:"N002 states that D is finite."},{location:"/inference/argument/3/produces/0/statement",reason:"F004 gives dim_H(F)≤q+1 for every real q>D in the n≥1 branch."},{location:"/inference/argument/0/produces/0/statement",reason:"F001 supplies both conclusions in the n=0 branch."}],justification:"Take q=D+1 to get dim_H(F)≤D+2<∞, so write its value as the finite nonnegative real d. If d>D+1, the real midpoint q=(D+d−1)/2 satisfies D<q<d−1, contradicting d≤q+1. The analogous argument with bounds d≤a for every a>1 gives d≤1 in S001. This expands the author's displayed finite-order contradiction and introduces neither a critical-exponent measure assertion nor a new analytic argument."}
